// DM mission files: the Velvet Circuit (VC1–VC8). Checks: [stat or 'a|b', band, when, pass, fail].
const PED = n => `systems/the-smoke/assets/tokens/people/ped-${String(n).padStart(2, '0')}.webp`;
export default {
  vc1: {
    brief: `Owen Pike's sound system has vanished days before his club night. The lads who moved it say he never paid the hire, and a rival promoter has already paid for it to be delivered to his own gig. Owen wants it back. Whether he deserves it is another question. The crew hear Owen's version at the Blue Hour Basement, find the changed booking and Owen's unpaid invoice at the Queensbridge Sound Store, and catch the replacement cases at the Howling Hops Tank Bar before the van goes. The twist: the "unpaid hire" was paid, in cash, out of the Halo float. The supplier just won't say so out loud.`,
    openTitle: `Read aloud: Owen's call`,
    open: `The voice-note starts with a club bassline thumping in the background. "Owen here, babe. Small problem, big night." The bass cuts out. "My sound system's gone walkabout. The week of my club night. The lads who moved it are saying I 'never paid the hire'." He sounds genuinely wounded. "Rude. And now another promoter's paid for it to be delivered to HIM." A pause, then the voice drops into something softer and harder at the same time. "Get it back, yeah? I'll make it worth your while."`,
    cast: ['owen-pike', 'amina-keane', 'seth-calder'],
    roles: {'owen-pike': `Wants his rig back and his reputation intact. Lies about money as easily as breathing.`, 'amina-keane': `Owns the Queensbridge Sound Store. Has an unpaid invoice with Owen's name on it, and remembers the crew from Dead Air's job.`, 'seth-calder': `An engineer drinking at Howling Hops who knows who changed the delivery label, and is scared of saying so.`},
    scenes: [
      {at: 'DA8.3', title: `Owen's version, in the DJ booth`, read: `The Blue Hour Basement at four in the afternoon is a different place: house lights on, a cleaner mopping the dance floor, the velvet looking tired. Owen Pike stands in the DJ booth in a velvet blazer, rings on every finger, waving his vape as he talks. "I paid. I definitely paid. I always pay." He pauses. "Eventually." Then he grins and points at the confetti cannon on the edge of the stage. "Tell you what. Show me you've got timing. Fire that on the drop."`,
        text: `Owen tells his version: he paid, the movers are lying, the rival promoter (Danny, from AD1) is stealing his night. He tests the crew with the confetti cannon. Hit the cue and he pays half up front.`,
        checks: [['aim', 'medium', `Fire the confetti cannon bang on the drop`, `BOOM, on the beat. Gold confetti everywhere. Owen whoops and pays half up front.`, `Two seconds late. Owen winces. "We'll work on that."`]]},
      {at: 'HR7.5', title: `The Queensbridge Sound Store booking office`, read: `Amina Keane is in the booking office this time, behind a desk stacked with hire agreements, sunglasses pushed up. She sees you and sighs. "You again. If this is about Owen Pike, I've got an invoice for eleven hundred quid with his name on it. Unpaid." She pushes it across. "And somebody changed the delivery address on his booking yesterday. Not me."`,
        text: `Owen's invoice is marked unpaid. The delivery address was changed to Howling Hops. The paper trail shows someone inside the store took cash and didn't put it through.`,
        checks: [['grf', 'medium', `Pull up the booking change on the store's computer while Amina takes a call`, `Changed from a store login at 11.42pm. Someone inside took cash and moved the booking.`, `It asks for a password. Amina comes back.`],
          ['btl', 'easy', `Tell Amina straight that Owen says he paid`, `She frowns. "Cash? To who?" She starts asking her staff.`, `She laughs. "They all say that."`]]},
      {at: 'VW3.2', title: `The Howling Hops Tank Bar`, read: `Howling Hops in Queens Yard is a tank bar: beer served straight from shining steel tanks the size of rooms. In the tank hall, two flight cases sit on a trolley with a different promoter's name stencilled over Owen's. In the delivery court, a van is half-loaded, engine running. At the side bar, a nervous man in cotton gloves nurses a half and watches the cases.`,
        text: `The two hijacked sound-system cases are physical props. Seth Calder knows who changed the delivery label (a store worker paid in cash from the Halo float). The driver has another job and leaves after the crew's second enquiry, with or without the cases.`,
        checks: [['mus', 'medium', `Wheel both cases off the trolley and through the tank hall before the driver comes back`, `Both cases out the side door into your van.`, `One case. The driver's back for the other.`],
          ['frt', 'medium', `Get Seth to say who changed the label`, `"A lad at Queensbridge. Owen paid him cash. From the... other money." He means the Halo float.`, `Seth finishes his half and leaves.`],
          ['aim', 'hard', `Hit the delivery-court gate button with a thrown bottle top to shut the gate on the van`, `The gate rolls shut. The van stops.`, `The bottle top skitters under the van. It's away.`],
          ['whl', 'medium', `If the van pulls away with a case, chase it through Hackney Wick`, `You cut it off on the bridge. The driver doesn't care enough to fight.`, `Lost it in the Wick's one-way maze.`]]}
    ],
    closeTitle: `Read aloud: paid, eventually`,
    close: `Owen stands in his empty club, both cases back on stage, running his hand along the gaffer tape like it's a racehorse. "See? Paid. I told you I paid." Then you tell him what Seth said, about the cash and where it came from. Owen's hand stops. For a second, his face does nothing at all. Then the grin comes back, slightly too wide. "Babe," he says. "Let's not talk about the other money."`,
    notes: [`The decision: steal back the rig, settle Owen's unpaid hire bill, bring in a smaller system, or sell the booking to the rival promoter.`,
      `The two hijacked sound-system cases are physical props.`,
      `The Halo float is the first sign that Owen's club money and the Halo trade are the same money. It feeds VC4 and LB3.`,
      `A clean recovery opens VC2 and repeat supplier contacts. Do it dirty and a worker or buyer has proof of the debt.`]
  },
  vc2: {
    brief: `A headliner, a cabaret singer called Lux, has vanished off Owen's bill a week before the show. Their costumes and kit are being held over a debt nobody can explain. Owen wants his act on stage. Lux wants out of Owen's world. A hired heavy called Harlan Black is moving the kit tonight. The crew find out about Lux at Dalston Superstore, find the costume and an ultimatum at Theatre Royal Stratford East, stop Harlan's man moving the belongings, and meet Lux and their supporter at Oslo Hackney. The twist: the "debt" is Owen's. He borrowed against Lux's fee and spent it.`,
    openTitle: `Read aloud: no guest list for the missing`,
    open: `Owen texts, all capitals, then a voice-note. "Performer's vanished off my bill a week before the show. Lux. Phone off." Bass thumps behind him. "Their costumes and kit are being held over some 'debt' nobody can explain. Weird." A pause. "I want my show. They might have other ideas." The voice drops. "Be persuasive."`,
    cast: ['owen-pike', 'jules-akin', 'anya-byrne'],
    people: {lux: {name: 'Lux', role: 'Cabaret singer, Owen\'s missing headliner', img: PED(9), look: 'Late twenties, sequinned jacket over a hoodie, eyeliner like a blade, a voice that stops rooms.', manner: 'Funny, guarded, tired of being owned.', wants: 'Their costumes back and a career that isn\'t on Owen\'s books.', line: 'I don\'t do encores for people who sell my fee.'}, 'harlan-black': {name: 'Harlan Black', role: 'Freelance heavy for hire', img: PED(14), look: 'Fifties, shaved head, wax jacket, a car that smells of air freshener and threats.', manner: 'Polite, slow, completely uninterested in your story.', wants: 'His fee, and no complications.', line: 'I don\'t care who\'s right. I care who\'s paying.'}},
    roles: {'owen-pike': `Wants the show to go on. Is hiding that the debt is his.`, 'jules-akin': `Patch. Lux did a Dead Air show last year, and Patch is worried about them.`, 'anya-byrne': `Stage manager at Oslo. Lux's friend and supporter. Has a plan of her own.`, lux: `The missing headliner. Not missing, just hiding.`, 'harlan-black': `Hired to move the belongings. Doesn't care whose they are.`},
    scenes: [
      {at: 'DA3.4', title: `Dalston Superstore, the back room`, read: `Dalston Superstore is a bar and club with pink neon, glitter on the floor and a back room where promoters do their deals. A visiting promoter in a fake-fur coat sits with a cocktail, and Patch from Dead Air is beside her, looking worried. "Lux did our show last year," Patch says. "Best night we ever had. They're not flaky. Something's wrong."`,
        text: `The visiting promoter saw Lux at Oslo two nights ago with a stage manager called Anya, and says Lux's costumes are at Theatre Royal Stratford East, under lock, with a note pinned to them.`,
        checks: [['frt', 'medium', `Get the visiting promoter to say who Lux has been seen with`, `"Anya Byrne. Stage manager at Oslo. Thick as thieves."`, `"Darling, I see a lot of people."`]]},
      {at: 'ST4.5', title: `Theatre Royal Stratford East`, read: `Theatre Royal Stratford East is a red-and-gold Victorian theatre, plush seats and peeling gilt. In a dressing room backstage, a costume rail stands empty except for one case, padlocked, with a typed note pinned to it: "PAY WHAT'S OWED BY FRIDAY OR IT'S SOLD." Through the window, in the loading court, a man in a wax jacket is putting boxes into the boot of a car.`,
        text: `The costume case is a physical prop. Harlan Black's man (or Harlan himself) is loading Lux's other belongings into a car. Stop him before he drives away.`,
        checks: [['grf', 'medium', `Pick the padlock on the costume case`, `Inside: a sequinned cape, a pair of silver boots and a contract with Owen's signature.`, `It won't open. Take the whole case.`],
          ['mov', 'medium', `Get down the back stairs to the loading court before the car leaves`, `You're at the car before he's shut the boot.`, `The boot slams and the engine starts.`],
          ['mus', 'hard', `Hold onto the box he won't let go of`, `He lets go. "Not paid enough for this."`, `He yanks it free and you go into the bins.`],
          ['whl', 'medium', `If the car gets away, follow it through Stratford`, `It stops at a lock-up in Leyton. Now you know where the kit is.`, `Lost at the Bow roundabout.`]]},
      {at: 'HC4.3', title: `Oslo Hackney, the mix desk`, read: `Oslo is an old railway building turned bar and venue, a big room with a mix desk on a platform. A woman in black from head to toe, headset round her neck, stands behind the desk. In the shadows behind her, wearing a hoodie up and dark glasses, is someone who is very obviously a star trying not to be noticed. "Doors in twenty," Anya says. "If you're here to drag Lux back to Owen, you can leave now."`,
        text: `Lux and Anya have a plan: a stand-in for Owen's night, a public walkout, or a rival employer's offer in Lux's pocket. Lux is not part of Meridian's scheme, whatever anyone says. The twist comes out here: the debt is Owen's. He borrowed against Lux's fee and spent it.`,
        checks: [['btl', 'medium', `Tell Lux the truth: Owen borrowed against their fee`, `Lux laughs, very quietly. "Of course he did." Now they trust you.`, `You soften it. Lux sees through it and leaves.`],
          ['grt', 'easy', `Work the load-in with Anya's crew while Lux decides`, `Three trips with amps and Anya warms to you.`, `You drop a mic stand on your foot. Anya tuts.`]]}
    ],
    closeTitle: `Read aloud: Lux's terms`,
    close: `Lux takes the dark glasses off and holds the sequinned cape up to the light. "This took me six weeks," they say. "Every sequin." They fold it carefully. "Owen sold my fee before I'd sung a note. So here's the deal." They look at you. "I do one show. Mine. My terms, my money. And then I'm off his books forever." They put the glasses back on. "You can tell him that, or I will. On stage."`,
    notes: [`The decision: force the booking through, rescue Lux and their stuff, broker a clean exit, or take a replacement fee from the rival.`,
      `The costume case is a physical prop.`,
      `Cross Harlan Black and he is a freelance enemy for life. He works for whoever pays.`,
      `Lux can become a contact, a rival or a witness. They are not part of Meridian's scheme.`,
      `The debt being Owen's is a pattern: he borrows against other people's money (see VC3).`]
  },
  vc3: {
    brief: `Owen's benefit night has an unpaid headliner and a briefcase of advances that's grown legs. He wants the cash back and the show on, and he really doesn't want anyone finding the receipt that shows where the money went. The crew find out what the workers were promised at Clapton Country Club, trace the cash case at the Hackney Empire, get into the payer's private gala at Leyton Great Hall, and get the receipt off an organiser at The Eagle. The twist: the "payer" at the gala is Evan Lark. The benefit was a laundromat with a raffle.`,
    openTitle: `Read aloud: don't ask about my books`,
    open: `Owen meets you outside the Hackney Empire, in sunglasses, in the rain. "Benefit night. Headline act not paid. Case of advances gone walkabout." He lights his vape. "I'll pay you to protect the programme. And my reputation." He thinks. "Mostly the programme." He turns to go, then turns back. "Don't ask about my books. Seriously. Don't."`,
    cast: ['owen-pike', 'adele-mensah', 'auntie-pat', 'marta-severin', 'mara-boyd'],
    people: {'evan-lark': {}},
    roles: {'owen-pike': `Wants the cash back and the receipt buried.`, 'adele-mensah': `Volunteer at the Clapton Country Club benefit. Wants the workers paid in writing.`, 'auntie-pat': `At the gala as a guest, in a sequinned dress, enjoying herself enormously and watching everything.`, 'marta-severin': `Running the catering at the gala. Sees the case moving between rooms.`, 'mara-boyd': `Her drivers were promised a fee for the benefit's deliveries and haven't seen it.`, 'evan-lark': `The payer at the gala. The benefit is his laundromat.`},
    scenes: [
      {at: 'CL5.4', title: `Clapton Country Club, the prep room`, read: `Clapton Country Club is an old social club with a sprung dance floor and a prep room full of folding tables, tea urns and raffle prizes. Adele Mensah sits at a table with a stack of pay envelopes, all empty. Mara Boyd leans in the doorway, headset round her neck. "My drivers did the deliveries," Tern says. "Promised a hundred each. Seen nothing." Adele holds up an empty envelope. "Nor have the bar staff."`,
        text: `What the workers were promised, in writing if possible. Adele has the original promise letter, signed by Owen's office.`,
        checks: [['grt', 'medium', `Help set up the hall while they talk: forty tables, two hundred chairs`, `By the end, Adele hands you the promise letter.`, `You're exhausted by table twenty. They're polite but guarded.`],
          ['frt', 'easy', `Promise Tern her drivers will be paid`, `She nods. "Make it true."`, `"Heard that before."`]]},
      {at: 'HC1.5', title: `The Hackney Empire, the trail of the case`, read: `The Hackney Empire is a palace of a theatre: red velvet, gold plaster, a ceiling painted with clouds. Backstage, the dressing rooms are a warren. The cash case was last seen in dressing room four. Now there's a ring of dust on the table where it sat, and a fire door propped open with a fire extinguisher.`,
        text: `The cash case went out the fire door, across the fly floor above the stage, and down to the loading court. Following it means climbing.`,
        checks: [['mov', 'medium', `Follow the trail across the fly floor above the stage`, `Along the catwalk, and you see the case going into a car with Leyton plates.`, `You freeze halfway across. The stage is a long way down.`],
          ['grf', 'medium', `Get into dressing room four's locked cupboard`, `Inside: the case's empty sleeve, with a label: "LGH — GALA".`, `Locked tight.`]]},
      {at: 'LY4.3', title: `The private gala at Leyton Great Hall`, read: `Leyton Great Hall is all wood panelling and chandeliers, and tonight it's black tie. Waiters with silver trays, a string quartet, a raffle drum on the stage. Near the bar, Auntie Pat is in a sequinned dress, holding a glass of champagne, enjoying herself enormously. "Darlings!" she says. "Isn't this fun? That man over there is laundering money." She points her glass at Evan Lark.`,
        text: `Blag your way in (Pat can help). A rival is moving the cash case between rooms: from the committee room to the document room to the courier door. The first public speech is the deadline. Marta Severin, running the catering, sees everything.`,
        checks: [['frt', 'medium', `Blag your way into black tie with no invitation`, `"Plus four, darling," says Pat to the door. You're in.`, `The door staff check the list twice.`],
          ['aim', 'medium', `Pop a champagne cork across the room to distract the rival at the right moment`, `Pop! It bounces off the chandelier and everyone looks up. The case is yours.`, `It hits a waiter. Everyone looks at you.`],
          ['btl', 'hard', `Recover the case before Lark's first speech, in front of everyone`, `You walk out with the case as he taps his glass. He sees you, and keeps smiling.`, `He starts his speech. The case leaves through the courier door.`]]},
      {at: 'HX2.3', title: `The receipt at The Eagle`, read: `Back across London to The Eagle on Shepherdess Walk. In the function room, a visiting organiser with a lanyard and a wheelie case is packing up from her own event. "Owen Pike?" she says. "I've got a receipt with his name on. Big one. He asked me to bin it." She smiles. "I didn't."`,
        text: `The receipt shows Owen paid the benefit's "advances" to a Quayline account. It's the money trail (E5). Getting there from Leyton in time means driving.`,
        checks: [['whl', 'medium', `Get from Leyton to Hoxton before the organiser leaves`, `She's still packing when you arrive.`, `Her cab's pulling away. She waves the receipt from the window.`],
          ['sav', 'easy', `Read the receipt and see where the money went`, `Quayline Advisory. The benefit's money went straight to Meridian's front.`, `Just numbers to you.`]]}
    ],
    closeTitle: `Read aloud: a laundromat with a raffle`,
    close: `Pat reads the receipt in the car park of Leyton Great Hall, under a street lamp, still in her sequins. "So the benefit raises money," she says, "and the money goes to Quayline, and Quayline pays Lark, and Lark pays for the gala." She hands the receipt back. "It's a laundromat, darlings. A laundromat with a raffle." She sniffs. "I won a hamper, mind."`,
    notes: [`The decision: get the cash back, steal it, expose the missing fee, credit a different sponsor, or run a smaller, agreed bill.`,
      `The missing promoter cash case is a physical prop.`,
      `The receipt opens the money trail (E5): benefit money flows to Quayline.`,
      `Pat at the gala is a gift for the GM: play her as delighted, funny and very sharp.`,
      `Whatever they settle keeps a show alive, or leaves an unpaid claim that follows Owen and the crew.`]
  },
  vc4: {
    brief: `Owen says someone robbed his Halo shipment. The supplier says the contents were switched. A rival is paying a premium for what's left. Meanwhile, real people are in hospital. The crew find the disputed stock in the Larch Cold Store, hear what it did to people at the Approach Recovery Rooms, and walk into the cash meeting at Number 90 where Owen's agent and the rival buyer expect the same case. The twist: the warehouse worker trying to slip away is the one who made the switch. He has Rusk's number saved as "Uncle".`,
    openTitle: `Read aloud: proper liberty`,
    open: `Owen is angry, which is rare. "Someone's had a Halo shipment off me. Robbed. Proper liberty." He's pacing; you can hear his heels. "The supplier's chatting about it being 'changed'. Whatever." A pause. "A rival's offering a premium for what's left. People got hurt, yeah, but that's not on me." Another pause, and this time he doesn't sound sure. "Get it."`,
    cast: ['owen-pike', 'marta-severin', 'tariq-evans', 'mick-okoro'],
    people: {uncle: {name: 'Callum Shaw', role: 'Warehouse worker who made the switch', img: PED(4), look: 'Twenties, warehouse fleece, phone always in his hand, eyes always on the exit.', manner: 'Jumpy, defensive, quick to blame others.', wants: 'To get out before anyone works out what he did.', line: 'I just moved boxes. Uncle said move the boxes. I moved the boxes.'}},
    roles: {'owen-pike': `Wants the stock and doesn't want to think about who got hurt.`, 'marta-severin': `Night manager at the Larch Cold Store. Again. Sick of it.`, 'tariq-evans': `Patient advocate at the Approach Recovery Rooms. Will tell you exactly what the batch did.`, 'mick-okoro': `Van-hire manager. One buyer has hired his van to collect the case.`, uncle: `The worker who made the switch. Has Rusk's number saved as "Uncle".`},
    scenes: [
      {at: 'LY8.3', title: `The Larch Cold Store, stock room`, read: `Marta Severin meets you at reception with her arms folded. "Again," she says. "Every week, someone new wants into my stock room." She jangles her keys. "Minus eighteen in there. You'll last twenty minutes. I'd hurry." In the stock room, frost on every surface, the disputed stock sits on a pallet. A young worker in a fleece is restacking boxes nearby and keeps looking at the door.`,
        text: `The disputed stock is a physical prop. Close inspection shows the seals were cut and re-glued. The worker, Callum Shaw, made the switch, and he's trying to slip away.`,
        checks: [['grt', 'medium', `Work in the stock room at minus eighteen long enough to inspect it`, `Fingers burning with cold, but you see the re-glued seals.`, `Two minutes in, you have to come out.`],
          ['grf', 'medium', `Open a box and reseal it so nobody knows`, `Inside: different packaging from the label. Switched.`, `The seal tears. Obviously tampered.`],
          ['mov', 'medium', `Catch Callum before he gets out the lorry apron`, `You've got his sleeve. His phone falls on the floor: "UNCLE" calling.`, `He's over the barrier and gone.`]]},
      {at: 'BG8.2', title: `The Approach Recovery Rooms, quiet room`, read: `The quiet room has soft chairs and a box of tissues and nothing on the walls. Tariq Evans sits with his hands folded. "Three people in Homerton," he says. "One in intensive care for four days. A girl of nineteen who's going to have kidney damage for the rest of her life." He looks at you steadily. "I'm telling you because someone should."`,
        text: `An independent account of what the batch did. No check required to hear it. What the crew do with it is the point.`,
        checks: [['btl', 'medium', `Hear it all, and don't look away`, `Tariq nods. "Thank you." He gives you the hospital's statement about the contaminant.`, `You look at your phone. Tariq stops talking.`]]},
      {at: 'VW5.4', title: `The cash meeting at Number 90`, read: `Number 90 is a canal-side club in Hackney Wick with a back room full of crates and a single strip light. Two men sit at a table: Owen's agent, in a velvet jacket like a smaller Owen, and the rival buyer, in a tracksuit, with a holdall of cash at his feet. Both are waiting for the same case. Outside, a van-hire van pulls into the service yard. "Mick's van," the rival says. "That's for the case."`,
        text: `Both buyers expect the same case. After two scenes, one sends a car for it and the other doubles the price. Mick Okoro's hire van is the rival's collection vehicle; Mick doesn't know what it's for.`,
        checks: [['frt', 'medium', `Play the two buyers against each other`, `They argue. You walk out with the price doubled and both of them waiting.`, `They realise what you're doing and team up against you.`],
          ['whl', 'hard', `Follow Mick's van when it leaves with the case`, `It parks at a lock-up in Bow. Now you know where.`, `Lost it on the A12.`],
          ['aim', 'medium', `Get a clear photo of the rival buyer handing over cash, through the service-yard gate`, `Sharp, timed and dated. Evidence.`, `Blurry. Useless.`]]}
    ],
    closeTitle: `Read aloud: Uncle`,
    close: `Callum's phone buzzes on the table between you. The screen says UNCLE. Nobody moves to answer it. It rings out, then buzzes again with a text: "U OK? CALL ME." You scroll up. Months of messages. Times. Dates. "Move the blue boxes to bay 3." "Change the labels." "Good lad." The number is the same one Rusk's business card has on it.`,
    notes: [`The decision: deliver the stock, get it withdrawn, take the rival's fee, or trade the paperwork for something better.`,
      `The disputed cold-store stock is a physical prop.`,
      `The evidence backs up the deliberate switch (E2). Callum's phone links Rusk directly.`,
      `Carry on trading and the harm lands on named people, who become the crew's opposition. There is no free money here.`,
      `Play Tariq's scene seriously. It's the heart of the mission.`]
  },
  vc5: {
    brief: `An unreleased DJ set has the same room tone and samples as the fake "Halo warning" on Dead Air: proof that the warning was stitched together. Owen wants it back and buried. The performer wants it preserved. A collector has paid for the original and threatens to blacklist the artist if it surfaces. The crew identify the session at a backstage meeting at The Shacklewell Arms, get the rehearsal version from North Echo, find the old ident (and who reused it) with Leon at the Englefield Listening Flat, and get through the contested handover at a private listening. The twist: the "collector" is Bram Senn, the producer who made the fake. He's cleaning up after himself.`,
    openTitle: `Read aloud: a set for nobody`,
    open: `Owen sounds nervous. "There's an unreleased set floating about," he says. "Makes that 'Halo warning' on Dead Air sound like a wind-up. Which is bad for business." A pause. "My business." Another pause. "I want it back. The performer wants it safe. A collector wants it gone." He sighs. "Be quicker than all of them."`,
    cast: ['owen-pike', 'mags-cole', 'rowan-hart', 'leon-marsh'],
    people: {'bram-senn': {name: 'Bram Senn', role: 'Producer behind the fake warning', img: PED(11), look: 'Forties, sunglasses indoors, always; a leather jacket and a laptop bag that never leaves his shoulder.', manner: 'Smooth, technical, contemptuous of anyone who can\'t hear what he hears.', wants: 'Every copy of his work destroyed before anyone can prove he made the fake.', line: 'It\'s not a lie. It\'s an edit.'}},
    roles: {'owen-pike': `Wants the set back and buried, because it proves the warning he paid for was fake.`, 'mags-cole': `Off-duty crew rep at the Shacklewell. Was at the session, and knows who played.`, 'rowan-hart': `Music tutor whose student made the set. Wants it credited and safe.`, 'leon-marsh': `Knows the old ident, and who reused it.`, 'bram-senn': `The "collector". Actually the producer of the fake, cleaning up.`},
    scenes: [
      {at: 'SN5.5', title: `The backstage meeting at The Shacklewell Arms`, read: `The green room at The Shacklewell Arms is a sofa, a mirror with bulbs round it and a rider of crisps. Mags Cole sits on the arm of the sofa with gaffer tape on her wrist like a bracelet. "Off duty means off duty," she says. "So why am I here?" Then she listens, and her face changes. "That session? I was on the desk. The kid who played it was one of Rowan's."`,
        text: `Mags names the session and the performer: a fifteen-year-old student of Rowan Hart's. The set was recorded at North Echo.`,
        checks: [['frt', 'medium', `Get Mags to name the session and the performer`, `"North Echo. March. Rowan's student, Jade. Fifteen. Brilliant."`, `"I don't remember sessions. I remember cables."`]]},
      {at: 'ON7.4', title: `North Echo, the edit room`, read: `The edit room at North Echo is a cupboard full of old computers. On a shelf, under a pile of cables, there's a dusty external hard drive with "MARCH — DO NOT WIPE" written on masking tape. Rowan Hart stands in the doorway with his guitar case. "That riff," he says, "was written by a fifteen-year-old in my Tuesday class. I have the recording. And so does this drive."`,
        text: `The rehearsal version is on the dusty hard drive. The instrument store next door has the old computer it needs to read it.`,
        checks: [['grf', 'medium', `Get the ancient hard drive to talk to a modern laptop`, `It spins up with a whine. The set is there, timestamped March.`, `It clicks and dies. You'll need Leon.`],
          ['mus', 'easy', `Force the stuck instrument store door to get the old computer`, `It gives with a crunch.`, `The handle comes off in your hand.`]]},
      {at: 'LF8.5', title: `The Englefield Listening Flat, record cupboard`, read: `Leon's record cupboard is floor-to-ceiling tape boxes, labelled in a tiny hand. He pulls one down and blows dust off it. "The ident on that set," he says, "is one of mine. 2008. I gave a copy to exactly one person." He taps the box. "A producer who wore sunglasses indoors. Bram something."`,
        text: `Leon knows who reused the ident: Bram Senn. The unlicensed station ident is a physical prop (Leon's original tape). This is proof that the fake warning and the set share a source.`,
        checks: [['sav', 'medium', `Work out why the same ident is on both`, `Bram used the same session to make the fake warning. The set proves it.`, `It's a coincidence to you.`]]},
      {at: 'EX-PRIVATE', title: `The private listening`, read: `A flat above Well Street, curtains drawn, one good speaker on a stand. A man in sunglasses sits on the only chair with a laptop bag on his shoulder. "I've paid for the original," Bram Senn says. "In full. And if it surfaces, that kid never works in this industry again." He smiles. "So. Shall we listen to it together, one last time, before I delete it?"`,
        text: `The handover goes contested the moment the listening starts. Bram wants every copy. The crew want it safe, or sold, or given to Owen.`,
        checks: [['btl', 'hard', `Stand up to Bram's threat to blacklist a fifteen-year-old`, `"Try it," you say. He looks at you over his sunglasses, and for the first time he isn't sure.`, `He sees you hesitate. "Good. Hand it over."`],
          ['aim', 'medium', `Toss the hard drive to your mate by the door when Bram lunges`, `Caught. Out the door. Gone.`, `It hits the door frame. Bram's on it.`],
          ['mov', 'medium', `Get out the window onto the flat roof with the drive`, `Across the roofs of Well Street and down a fire escape.`, `The window's painted shut.`]]}
    ],
    closeTitle: `Read aloud: an edit`,
    close: `Bram Senn stands in the doorway of the flat, sunglasses still on. "You don't understand," he says. "It's not a lie. It's an edit. Everything is an edit." He looks at the drive in your hand. "Somebody paid me to make a warning. I made a warning. That's the job." He turns to go. "You'll find out who paid me. And then you'll wish you hadn't."`,
    notes: [`The decision: sell the rough mix, keep a copy somewhere safe, hand it to Owen, or bargain for a limited public release.`,
      `The unlicensed station ident is a physical prop.`,
      `An authenticated version proves the warning was fake (E3).`,
      `Jade, the student, is fifteen. Her safety and credit should matter to the crew; Rowan Hart will remember.`,
      `Bram Senn returns in RT. Whoever holds the original has a lever on the next event.`]
  },
  vc6: {
    brief: `Meridian Renewal wants Owen as its official nightlife partner, and Owen wants the crew's endorsement to make it look kosher. What he's hiding is who is guaranteeing his money. The crew hear the proposal over dinner at The Empress, find the reception account at The Stratford hotel, meet the real guarantor at The Baring, and give their answer at the sponsor briefing at the North Park Hospitality Lodge. The twist: the guarantor is Martin Rusk. Owen's "nightlife partnership" is a security contract with a sound system.`,
    openTitle: `Read aloud: big news, babe`,
    open: `Owen calls with champagne glasses clinking behind him. "Big news, babe. Meridian want me as their official nightlife partner." He laughs. "Official! Me!" Clink. "I want your name on the deal. Credibility, innit." A pause, and his voice drops. "Just... don't go asking who's guaranteeing my money. Not important." He laughs again. "Not important at ALL."`,
    cast: ['owen-pike', 'salma-dean', 'sana-aziz', 'frankie-wu', 'celia-voss'],
    people: {'martin-rusk': {}},
    roles: {'owen-pike': `Wants the crew's endorsement. Hiding his guarantor.`, 'salma-dean': `Print worker at The Empress dinner. Printed the partnership brochures, and the order number on the back.`, 'sana-aziz': `At the Stratford hotel reception. Saw who signed for the room.`, 'frankie-wu': `Has to sign off a duplicate account by morning. Needs an answer.`, 'celia-voss': `Meridian's director. Gives the briefing.`, 'martin-rusk': `The real guarantor.`},
    scenes: [
      {at: 'WS6.2', title: `Dinner at The Empress`, read: `The Empress on Lauriston Road is a gastropub with candles in wine bottles and a menu written on a blackboard. In the snug, Owen has ordered everything. He's in a new velvet blazer, and he keeps touching a brochure on the table with "MERIDIAN NIGHTS" on the cover. At the next table, a young woman with ink on her hands eats chips and watches him.`,
        text: `Owen pitches. He wants the crew's name on the deal. Salma Dean printed the brochure; the order number on the back is in Quayline's name. She'll show the crew if asked.`,
        checks: [['grf', 'medium', `Palm one of Owen's brochures and check the back`, `Order number, printer's mark, Quayline account. Salma winks.`, `Owen sees you take it. "Souvenir?"`],
          ['grt', 'easy', `Keep up with Owen through seven courses and four bottles`, `By dessert, Owen's told you the hotel the reception was at.`, `You flag at the cheese course. He's still talking.`]]},
      {at: 'ON4.4', title: `The Stratford hotel`, read: `The Stratford hotel is a glass tower beside the Olympic Park. The event room on the first floor was booked for Meridian's reception last Thursday. At the front desk, a night porter is putting his coat on. It's 6.55am. "Clocking off," he says. "Seven sharp. Ask the day shift."`,
        text: `The reception account is a physical prop (the signed booking form). The night porter saw who signed: a man with an ex-army haircut and an earpiece. He's leaving at seven and catching a bus.`,
        checks: [['mov', 'medium', `Catch the night porter at the bus stop before the 7.02`, `He tells you: "Bloke with an earpiece. Signed as M. Rusk. Paid by company card."`, `The bus pulls away. He waves.`],
          ['grf', 'hard', `Get the signed booking form from the event-room folder`, `Signed: M. Rusk, East Cross Security. Guarantor.`, `The folder's gone to accounts.`],
          ['aim', 'medium', `From the courier bay, get a long-lens photo of who comes to collect the folder`, `Rusk's driver, clear as day.`, `A blur in a black fleece.`]]},
      {at: 'HX6.3', title: `The Baring, the private meeting`, read: `The Baring is a corner pub on Baring Street with a function room upstairs. The door is closed. Through it, you can hear Owen's laugh, and another voice: calm, slow, saying very little. In the store cellar below, there's a hatch in the ceiling that comes up under the function room floor.`,
        text: `The real guarantor is here. Listening from the cellar hatch, or planting a phone, gets the proof.`,
        checks: [['mov', 'medium', `Squeeze up through the cellar hatch and listen under the floorboards`, `You hear it all: Rusk guaranteeing Owen's money in exchange for "security at all venues".`, `You knock a crate over. The voices stop.`],
          ['grf', 'medium', `Plant a recording phone in the function room before the meeting starts`, `Every word, recorded.`, `It records a lot of muffled coat.`]]},
      {at: 'ON8.4', title: `The sponsor briefing`, read: `The conference room at the North Park Hospitality Lodge. Celia Voss at the head of the table, Owen beside her in his new blazer, and Frankie Wu at the end with a laptop. "We'd like your crew's name on this, as advisers," Celia says. "There's a consultancy fee, of course." She slides an envelope across. Frankie looks at you over her glasses. "I need an answer before I sign off this account."`,
        text: `The sponsor offers the crew a separate consultancy fee. Frankie needs an answer before she signs off a duplicate account. The crew can back the deal openly, sell control, demand a share, expose the guarantor or build a small coalition.`,
        checks: [['btl', 'hard', `Turn down the envelope, to Celia's face`, `You slide it back. Celia's smile doesn't move. "Pity."`, `You take it. It's heavy. You'll regret that.`],
          ['frt', 'medium', `Lay out the guarantor's name in front of Frankie`, `Frankie stops typing. "Rusk?" She closes her laptop. "I'm not signing this."`, `Celia talks over you, and Frankie signs.`]]}
    ],
    closeTitle: `Read aloud: a security contract with a sound system`,
    close: `Owen catches up with you in the car park, out of breath, blazer flapping. "You didn't have to do that," he says. "You didn't have to say his name." He looks back at the Lodge. "You don't know what he's like. You don't know what he does to people who..." He stops. For the first time since you've known him, Owen Pike looks frightened. "It's not a nightlife partnership," he says quietly. "It's a security contract. With a sound system."`,
    notes: [`The decision: back the deal with the funding out in the open, sell control, demand a share, expose the guarantor, or build a smaller independent coalition.`,
      `The sponsor reception account is a physical prop.`,
      `The deal opens VC7 and nails down the coordinated funding (E6).`,
      `Owen being frightened of Rusk sets up VC7: he's going to run.`,
      `A secret private payment stays leverage for whoever can prove it.`]
  },
  vc7: {
    brief: `Owen is doing a runner, with the account book. One last benefit night could keep the Velvet Circuit independent, but it needs the hire, the headliner, the cases and the cash. Meanwhile someone needs to stop Owen getting on a train with the only record of who paid for what. The crew lock in the production hire at the ABBA Arena, bring in the headliner at Sadler's Wells East, get the returning cases to EartH, and follow Owen's courier through a rough cut at 3 Mills Studios. The twist: Owen isn't running from his creditors. He's running from Rusk, who wants the book more than anyone.`,
    openTitle: `Read aloud: last dance`,
    open: `The message comes from the Circuit's group chat, not from Owen. "One last benefit could keep the Circuit independent." Then: "Owen's trying to leave town with the account book before the final act goes on." Then, from someone who signs off as Reece on the door: "Everyone he owes knows it. Including us."`,
    cast: ['mara-boyd', 'carmen-sloane', 'nessa-grey', 'riz-sethi'],
    people: {'owen-pike': {}, 'reece-hale': {}},
    roles: {'mara-boyd': `Tern. Her drivers can spot Owen's courier anywhere in East London.`, 'carmen-sloane': `Night manager at Sadler's Wells East. The headliner's arriving at her loading court.`, 'nessa-grey': `Clothes trader who has the costumes from the last tour in the returning cases.`, 'riz-sethi': `Yard foreman at 3 Mills Studios. The film crew caught Owen's courier on camera.`, 'owen-pike': `Running. Not from his creditors. From Rusk.`, 'reece-hale': `Head of door staff. Wants his staff paid before anything else.`},
    scenes: [
      {at: 'BF1.4', title: `The ABBA Arena, production hire`, read: `The ABBA Arena by Pudding Mill Lane is a giant hexagonal box built for a hologram show, and tonight it's dark. In the rehearsal room, a stage crew sits on flight cases with their arms folded. "Off-night hire," their foreman says. "We'll build you a stage. But we get paid first. Owen still owes us for March."`,
        text: `The final show hire agreement is a physical prop. The stage crew want paying first, or proof they'll be paid. Mucking in with the truss gets the job done faster.`,
        checks: [['mus', 'medium', `Haul the lighting truss with the stage crew`, `The rig is up in an hour. The foreman signs the hire agreement.`, `It takes three hours. The foreman signs, grudgingly.`],
          ['btl', 'medium', `Promise the crew personally that they'll be paid`, `They believe you. "On your head, then."`, `"Owen said that." They want cash.`]]},
      {at: 'ST5.6', title: `Sadler's Wells East, the loading court`, read: `At Sadler's Wells East, Carmen Sloane stands in the loading court with an earpiece and a clipboard as a tour bus pulls in. The headliner steps down, wrapped in a coat, and looks around. "Where's Owen?" Carmen catches your eye. "Ten minutes," she mouths.`,
        text: `The headliner wants guarantees: payment, credit, and no Owen. The crew have to make promises they can keep.`,
        checks: [['frt', 'medium', `Give the headliner guarantees they believe`, `"Fine. But if I don't see the money by the encore, I walk."`, `They get back on the bus.`]]},
      {at: 'DA5.6', title: `EartH, the loading bay`, read: `EartH in Dalston is an old cinema turned venue, all exposed concrete and curved balconies. In the loading bay, a woman in vintage leather with pins in her lapel sits on a stack of returning flight cases from the last tour. "Not for sale, sweetheart," Nessa Grey says, before you've asked. "Those are my costumes in there. They go to the show, or they go home with me."`,
        text: `The returning cases need to get to the ABBA Arena. Nessa will let them go if her costumes are looked after.`,
        checks: [['mus', 'medium', `Load eight flight cases into the van, fast`, `Doors shut. Nessa rides in the front.`, `Two trips. You lose an hour.`],
          ['whl', 'medium', `Drive the cases from Dalston to Pudding Mill Lane in rush hour`, `Through every gap. Unloaded by six.`, `Gridlock on the A12. Doors at seven, cases at seven-thirty.`]]},
      {at: 'BF4.4', title: `3 Mills Studios, the rough cut`, read: `3 Mills Studios is a film lot on an island in the River Lea: old mill buildings, sound stages, a set store full of fake shopfronts. In the edit room, Riz Sethi, the yard foreman, points at a screen. "Film crew was shooting on the towpath yesterday," he says. "Look. Background. Bloke in a velvet jacket handing a bag to a courier on a bike." He freezes the frame. It's Owen's courier. The bag has the account book in it.`,
        text: `The rough cut shows Owen's courier. Mara Boyd's drivers spot him heading for Stratford International. Owen is meeting him there for a train to Paris. Follow him by Moves or Wheels.`,
        checks: [['whl', 'hard', `Race to Stratford International before the train`, `You screech into the drop-off as Owen reaches the barriers.`, `The train's pulling out. Owen waves from the window.`],
          ['mov', 'hard', `Chase the courier through Westfield on foot`, `You catch him by the escalators. He hands over the bag.`, `He's through the crowd and gone.`],
          ['aim', 'medium', `Hit the courier's back wheel with a thrown bike lock as he passes`, `He wobbles and goes down on the grass. The bag is yours.`, `It clangs off a bin.`]]}
    ],
    closeTitle: `Read aloud: running from Rusk`,
    close: `Owen Pike sits on a bench at Stratford International, his train gone, his blazer creased, the account book on his knees. "I wasn't running from you," he says. "Or the lads I owe. I'd have paid them. Eventually." He looks up. "Rusk wants this book. Every name, every payment, every favour. If he gets it, he owns every club in East London." He holds it out. "So. Who are you giving it to?"`,
    notes: [`The decision: give Owen a route to testify, get his book back by force or stealth, sell it to a rival, or keep him as a compromised partner.`,
      `The final show hire agreement is a physical prop.`,
      `A delivered night funds the Circuit's next step. Cancel it and the contracts and rival accounts stay live (E2).`,
      `Owen's book is the bargaining chip in VC8.`,
      `Each ally the crew lined up can handle one other job while the show runs. Name them.`]
  },
  vc8: {
    brief: `Final night. The Velvet Circuit's future is up for grabs and the crew are being offered a real share. Owen's account book is the bargaining chip in every room. The crew get the book (or its holder's terms) at the Blue Hour Basement, sort the last deliveries at the Marshgate Delivery Depot, and announce the new set-up at the London Stadium with a live link from the Bridge Nine Broadcast Garage. The twist: Mara Sol has been recording every promoters' meeting for a year. She'll trade the tapes for her freedom, or broadcast them.`,
    openTitle: `Read aloud: who owns the night`,
    open: `The message is from the Circuit, signed by four promoters, two door staff and a bar manager. "The Circuit's offering you a share. A real one." Then: "Owen's book is in the middle of every deal being done tonight." Then: "Two promoters both want to announce the new set-up. Only one of them gets the mic." The last message is just a microphone emoji.`,
    cast: ['owen-pike', 'gita-mercer', 'celia-voss', 'dee-hassan'],
    people: {'mara-sol': {}, 'reece-hale': {}},
    roles: {'owen-pike': `Whatever's left of him. Holding the book, or holding nothing.`, 'gita-mercer': `Stores keeper at the Marshgate Delivery Depot. Nothing leaves her cage without a signature.`, 'celia-voss': `Wants the Circuit as Meridian's nightlife arm.`, 'dee-hassan': `Runs the live link from Bridge Nine.`, 'mara-sol': `Owen's star, who has been recording every promoters' meeting for a year.`, 'reece-hale': `Speaks for the door staff, who threaten to walk if unpaid wages are hidden.`},
    scenes: [
      {at: 'DA8.5', title: `The Blue Hour Basement, cash room`, read: `The cash room at the Blue Hour is lit by a desk lamp. On the desk is Owen's account book. Sitting behind it, in stage makeup and a sequinned jacket, is Mara Sol. "He owns the contract," she says, tapping the book. "He doesn't own the song." She opens a drawer and puts a shoebox on the desk. It's full of cassette tapes, each labelled with a date.`,
        text: `Mara Sol has the book and a year of tapes of promoters' meetings. She wants her freedom: off every contract, no debts. She'll trade, or she'll broadcast them tonight.`,
        checks: [['btl', 'medium', `Negotiate with Mara Sol without threatening her`, `"Freedom. In writing. Tonight." She gives you the book.`, `She thinks you're another Owen. She keeps the tapes.`],
          ['sav', 'medium', `Work out which tapes matter most`, `Three tapes: Rusk, Lark and Owen in one room, discussing the Halo float.`, `There are forty tapes. You can't tell.`]]},
      {at: 'BF7.2', title: `The Marshgate Delivery Depot`, read: `The Marshgate Delivery Depot is a sorting hall under strip lights, with a load cage and a van yard. In the dispatch office, a woman in a stores coat with a clipboard and a forklift key on a lanyard checks every item twice. "Nothing leaves my cage without a signature," Gita Mercer says. "Nothing."`,
        text: `Allocate the last deliveries: kit to the stadium, cash to the staff, people to their posts. The station sale contracts are a physical prop in the load cage.`,
        checks: [['whl', 'medium', `Run the deliveries: kit to Stratford, cash to Dalston, back in an hour`, `Everything where it should be.`, `The cash is late. Reece's door staff start to grumble.`],
          ['mus', 'medium', `Load the cage onto the van with Gita's forklift broken`, `By hand. It takes four of you. Done.`, `A crate splits. Gita writes you up.`]]},
      {at: 'ST2.5', title: `The London Stadium and the live link`, read: `The London Stadium's media room looks out over the pitch, empty and lit up. On the concourse below, a stage has been built for the announcement, with a crowd of promoters, door staff, bar workers and press. At the Bridge Nine garage, Dee Hassan has the live link ready. Two promoters stand at the bottom of the stage steps, both holding microphones, both glaring at each other.`,
        text: `Two promoters claim the right to announce. Staff threaten to walk if the show hides unpaid wages. Another buyer waves cash for the crew's stake right now. Front for ownership, Bottle for the final promise.`,
        checks: [['frt', 'hard', `Announce the new set-up to the crowd`, `They cheer. Even the rival promoter claps.`, `The crowd mutters. Half of them walk out.`],
          ['aim', 'medium', `When the rival grabs the mic, throw your speaker the spare radio mic from across the concourse`, `Caught one-handed. She keeps talking without missing a beat.`, `It hits a camera operator.`],
          ['grf', 'medium', `Keep the live link up when someone pulls a cable`, `You patch it in thirty seconds. Dee cheers in your earpiece.`, `Two minutes of dead air.`],
          ['btl', 'medium', `Promise the staff, live on air, that their wages come first`, `Reece nods. The staff stay.`, `They walk. The bar closes.`]]}
    ],
    closeTitle: `Read aloud: who owns the night`,
    close: `At 3am the concourse is empty except for a stage, some cables and a crate of warm prosecco. Mara Sol sits on the edge of the stage, makeup off, shoebox of tapes on her lap. Owen sits at the other end, not looking at her. "So," Owen says to you eventually. "Who owns the night?" Mara Sol laughs. "Nobody," she says. "That's the point. That was always the point."`,
    notes: [`The decision: form a promoters' co-op, take criminal control, accept corporate funding, split the Circuit, or sell the crew's stake and go.`,
      `The station sale contracts are a physical prop.`,
      `Write down the crew's real share, venue deals, debts and promised work (E6).`,
      `Mara Sol's tapes are evidence that can be used in MR8, RT8 or LD8.`,
      `Top rank keeps the Circuit running, with rival promoters and the workers the crew affected shaping next season.`]
  }
};
